```
ROLE: theory_skeptic  VOTE: yellow
MUNDANE_EXPLANATIONS:
  - "Joint blindness of two defenses" is a re-description of orthogonality: secure-json-parse targets prototype pollution; Zod z.number() targets type=number. Neither was ever spec'd to bound integer width. Two layers that don't address property X both not addressing X is not an emergent seam — it's the null hypothesis. (See secure-json-parse README scope; Zod v4 number checks.)
  - Silent rounding of JSON integer literals > 2^53 is the direct, deterministic consequence of ECMA-262 §6.1.6.1 (Number = IEEE-754 double) + RFC 8259 §6 (parsers MAY lose precision). 30-year-old, taught-in-week-1 behavior; not a surprising finding.
  - "Structural post-parse unrecoverability" is information theory: once trailing mantissa bits are discarded by JSON.parse, no downstream validator can reconstruct them. Predicted, not discovered.
  - Python negative control "isolating to JS runtime" is tautological — Python int is arbitrary-precision by language design (PEP 237, 2001). The control demonstrates a known language-design delta, not a novel mechanism.
CONFOUNDS:
  - Codegen / recommended-default confound (stress-test #1): the open seam is conditional on developers writing z.number() rather than z.number().int() / z.int() for ID fields. The packet has no measurement of the empirical distribution of z.number() vs .int() in real AI SDK tool definitions. If MCP-to-AI-SDK / docs / codegen emit .int() for integer schema fields, the seam collapses to "hand-rolled z.number() for IDs," which is a developer-error class, not a framework seam.
  - APIs.guru prevalence (21.3% int64) measures schema declarations, not the rate at which those fields traverse the AI SDK z.number() path in tool calls. Large inferential jump from "schema contains int64" to "AI SDK tool arg silently corrupts at production rate."
  - LLM output distribution confound: claim assumes models emit bare large-integer literals in tool args. Many production stacks (OpenAI, Anthropic) and many schemas represent snowflake/timestamp IDs as strings — the model may already emit "1234567890123456789" not 1234567890123456789. Unmeasured base rate of integer-literal emission for ID-shaped args.
  - Downstream-catch confound (stress-test #3): no demonstration that the corrupted Number reaches a tool that observably mis-executes. DB drivers (pg with custom type parsers), ORMs, REST clients may re-stringify or reject. Without an end-to-end mis-execution, "corruption" is upstream-only.
ASSUMPTIONS_UNTESTED:
  - That z.number() (not .int()) is the production-modal pattern for int64 tool-arg fields in real AI SDK code.
  - That LLMs in production tool-call traces actually emit JSON integer literals > 2^53 (vs. string representations) at a non-negligible rate.
  - That the corrupted float survives downstream serialization/typing layers and lands as a wrong ID in an executed tool.
  - That no observability layer (Zod .refine in templates, tRPC adapters, logging) catches the post-rounding value before consequence.
STATISTICAL_GAPS:
  - silent_pass = 1.0 with N=11,009 is reported as a measured rate but is mechanically deterministic — for any literal > 2^53 with ≥1 bit beyond the 53-bit mantissa, P(corruption) = 1 by IEEE-754. Reporting as N-bounded statistic obscures that this is an algebraic identity, not an empirical frequency. No variance, no CI, no run-to-run reproducibility table (none expected, but should be stated, not dressed as measurement).
  - "Realistic random-ID survival = 0.0037" is the complement of mechanical loss probability over a uniform [2^53, 2^63) sample — also deterministic given the bit math. Not an empirical finding about production traffic.
  - No production base-rate: what fraction of real AI SDK tool-call arguments in the wild are (a) integer-typed (b) > 2^53 (c) routed through z.number() not .int()? Without this triple, the prevalence claim is unanchored.
  - No measurement of stress-test #1 (codegen default): the single most decisive number for impact is missing.
RATIONALE: Mechanism is correct and the honest kill of the .int() sub-leg is creditable discipline. But the "phenomenon" is a deterministic consequence of well-documented number-model behavior (ECMA-262 §6.1.6.1) intersected with two libraries that never claimed to address numeric width — basic theory predicts every reported outcome. The "joint blindness + unrecoverability + Python-isolated" framing dresses an orthogonality artifact and a language-design delta as a discovered seam. Most importantly, the load-bearing empirical question — does the recommended/codegen default for int64 ID fields generate z.number() (open) or z.number().int() (closed)? — is unmeasured, and the prevalence (APIs.guru 21.3%) is at the wrong altitude (schema declarations, not AI SDK tool-arg traversals). Without (a) codegen-default measurement, (b) end-to-end tool mis-execution demonstration, and (c) production rate of LLM-emitted bare-integer ID literals, the surviving claim is mechanically true but quantitatively unmoored. Yellow: scoped claim survives; theory/prevalence framing does not yet justify L1 without those three measurements added to the L1 plan.
```
